. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 * 
. * This section tests whether having daughters leads to a larger increase in s
> upport for 
. * gender equality among men compared to women (H4). 
. * 
.                 * Dependent Variable:  
.                 * As in Hypothesis 1, we use the item plh0174, where responde
> nts report their satisfaction 
.                 * with the current division of housework.  
.                 * We construct the binary variable satis_housew, coded as 1 i
> f the respondent reports 
.                 * high satisfaction (values 8–10), and 0 otherwise. A value o
> f 1 is interpreted as 
.                 * an attitudinal indicator of perceived fairness in the domes
> tic arrangement, 
.                 * and thus as a proxy for a supportive stance toward gender-b
> alanced domestic roles.
.                 * This assumes that satisfaction reflects personal approval o
> f the arrangement, 
.                 * not necessarily objective equality.
.                 * 
.                 * Model Specification:  
.                 * We estimate a logistic regression to test whether having da
> ughters influences support 
.                 * for gender equality differently for men and women. To test 
> this gender-specific 
.                 * heterogeneity, we include a continuous measure of the numbe
> r of daughters 
.                 * (daughters_nr) and an interaction term with the respondent'
> s gender (d_male). 
.                 * Total number of children fixed-effects (i.tot_kids) are inc
> luded to control for 
.                 * family size, and standard errors are clustered at the respo
> ndent level to account 
.                 * for repeated observations in the panel. The model is specif
> ied as follows:  
. 
. logit satis_housew c.daughters_nr##d_male `controls' i.tot_kids, cluster(pid)

note: 12.tot_kids != 0 predicts success perfectly;
      12.tot_kids omitted and 13 obs not used.

